<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wilhelm Frick</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1877">1877</a>–<a href="/years/1946">1946</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wilhelm Frick was a leading Nazi politician. In 1930 he became interior minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>, the first Nazi to hold a ministerial post in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, and from 1933 to 1943 he was Reich Minister of the Interior, drafting laws that centralised the state and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a> race laws. He was hanged after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg-trials">Nuremberg trials</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wilhelm-frick.jpg" alt="Wilhelm Frick" width="320" height="415" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Frick_912_(3x4_cropped)_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Frick was born in Alsenz in the Palatinate, then part of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, the youngest of four children of a Protestant teacher. He studied philology in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> and then law at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, receiving his doctorate in 1901, and joined the Bavarian civil service in 1903. Rejected as unfit, he did not serve in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. As head of the political <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of the Munich police from 1923 he took part in Hitler's Beer Hall Putsch on 9 November, and in April 1924 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-court-germany">People's Court</a> gave him a suspended sentence of 15 months for aiding and abetting high treason.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Elected to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag">Reichstag</a> in May 1924, he joined the re-established <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazi-party">Nazi Party</a> on 1 September 1925 and became its parliamentary group leader in 1928. On 23 January 1930 he was appointed to the Thuringian ministries of the Interior and Education, the first Nazi to hold a ministerial post at any level in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. He dismissed Communist and Social Democratic officials, banned newspapers and anti-war films, and was removed by a motion of no confidence on 1 April 1931.</p><p>He became Reich Minister of the Interior in Hitler's cabinet on 30 January 1933. His ministry at first had no authority over the police, but his power grew with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag-fire-decree">Reichstag Fire Decree</a> and the Enabling Act. He drafted many of the Gleichschaltung laws, including the Law Against the Formation of Parties of 14 July 1933, and laws of racial policy, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg-laws">Nuremberg Laws</a> of September 1935. His power was greatly reduced in June 1936 when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-himmler">Heinrich Himmler</a> became Chief of German Police.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Himmler replaced him as Interior Minister in August 1943. Frick then became Protector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a>, where he used ruthless methods to counter dissent. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg-trials">Nuremberg trials</a> he refused to testify on his own behalf. He was convicted of planning and waging wars of aggression, war crimes and crimes against humanity, including for his role in formulating the Enabling Act and the Nuremberg Laws, and was sentenced to death on 1 October 1946. He was hanged at Nuremberg Prison on 16 October, and his body was cremated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> and the ashes scattered in the river <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isar">Isar</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wilhelm Frick” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Nazi writers such as <b>Wilhelm Frick</b> and Alfred Rosenberg labelled it “un-German”, stirred controversy over flat roofs and painted it as a front for communists, and each move and change of leadership followed political pressure.</blockquote><a href="/movement/bauhaus">Bauhaus · Outcomes</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1930 <b>Wilhelm Frick</b> became Thuringia's interior minister, the first Nazi to hold a ministerial post.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-state-of-thuringia">Free State of Thuringia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Alsenz, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Nuremberg Court Prison, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.73,7.81&amp;year=1921" aria-label="Alsenz on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Wilhelm Frick"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5431 5848h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5431 5848h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.73,7.81&amp;year=1921">What was here in 1921? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
